In `src/snapshot.rs`, the `try_update` method uses `arc-swap`'s `rcu()` to conditionally swap to a newer snapshot. The current implementation mutates an `applied` variable inside the closure to track whether the swap happened:

```rust
pub fn try_update(&self, new_snap: Arc<Snapshot>) -> bool {
    let mut applied = false;
    self.current.rcu(|current| {
        if new_snap.version > current.version {
            applied = true;  // BUG: closure may run multiple times
            Arc::clone(&new_snap)
        } else {
            Arc::clone(current)
        }
    });
    applied
}
```

This is a concurrency bug: `rcu()`'s closure is a compare-and-swap retry loop and may run more than once. Fix `try_update` so the 'was applied' result is derived correctly from `rcu()`'s return value (the previous Arc), not from any variable mutated inside the closure.

